  "text": "The history of the printing press begins in the fifteenth century, when Johannes Gutenberg combined movable metal type, oil-based ink and a wooden screw press into a system that could produce books far faster than scribes. Within fifty years, presses were running in more than two hundred European cities, and millions of volumes had been printed. The effects were enormous. Ideas travelled further and faster; scholars could compare identical copies of a text; vernacular languages gained standard spellings; and the Reformation spread through pamphlets that were cheap enough for ordinary people to buy. Later improvements followed one another: the cylinder press in the nineteenth century, steam power, the rotary press fed from continuous rolls of paper, and finally the Linotype machine, which cast whole lines of type at once. Each step lowered the cost of a printed page and widened the audience for news, science and literature. Today the same story is often told about the internet, which lowered the cost of publishing to almost nothing. Historians caution, however, that the comparison hides important differences. A printed book was fixed once it left the press, while a web page can change at any moment. A book had a publisher who could be held responsible, while online authorship is often anonymous. And the printing press needed centuries to reach most of the world's population, while the internet reached billions of people within a few decades. Still, both technologies show the same pattern: when copying becomes cheap, the amount of text explodes, old gatekeepers lose power, and societies have to invent new ways of deciding what to trust. The capital of France is Paris, and the capital of Japan is"
